#
# **What this notebook is.** The bell-transport-bench repository, compressed into one self-contained file so the benchmark runs anywhere, Colab included, without a clone. The repository is where the work is done: a teammate drives it with `make` commands, shown under "How the repository works" below. This notebook packs the repository's package, its recorded runs and its config files, writes each config file out where it explains it, remakes every run it reports, and sets each beside the run the repository recorded. Every config file, circuit and result below is the repository's own, carried here.
#
# **Overview.** Under one simulated noise model, our protection raised the fidelity F of a Bell pair between two distant qubits on all three processor graphs, keeping at least {{ pct([c[3], c[6], c[9]] | map(attribute="acceptance") | min) }} of shots and using fewer two-qubit gates than the organisers' baseline. [Section {{ sec("s5-1") }}]({{ ref("s5-1") }}) gives each graph's numbers.{{ chk() }}
#
# - **The task:** create the Bell state |β₀₀⟩ = (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2, which the challenge writes |Φ+⟩, between two fixed qubits, the ports, on three processor graphs, and measure what error detection with postselection buys and costs.
# - **Our protection:** GHZ disentangling moves the pair by preparing a GHZ state along the path between the ports and disentangling it with gates, which leaves the path's interior qubits in |0⟩. The vacancy read then reads those qubits at the end of the circuit and discards each shot where any reads 1. It detects errors and corrects none.
# - **The noise:** one model from IBM's Boston calibration medians, the same on every graph, simulated with {{ "{:,}".format(B.shots) }} shots per basis. Every result holds for this one model.
