# %% [markdown]
# ### {{ H["s4-4"] }}
#
# **Each run is one circuit file: an OpenQASM 3 circuit on the graph's physical qubits, and how its measured bits are read.** One transport and one check, besides the baseline, fill the nine cases:
#
# - **Baseline (cases 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8):** the organisers' recipe, H and CX at one port, SWAPs out to the other port and back, each SWAP three CX gates.
# - **GHZ disentangling, our transport:** H on the first port, then a chain of CX gates along the path copies its bit onto every qubit, giving the GHZ state (|0…0⟩ + |1…1⟩)/√2 on the whole path. A second chain of CX gates walks back towards the first port, each gate controlled by the qubit before it, and returns every interior qubit to |0⟩, so only the two ports keep the shared bit: the Bell pair. The last of these gates is controlled by the first port. Liao et al. build a long-range CNOT the same way, preparing a GHZ state and disentangling it with gates ([arXiv:2411.14638](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.14638)).{{ chk() }}
# - **Vacancy read (cases 3, 6, 9):** after the transport, each interior qubit is measured in the Z basis at the end of the circuit. Each should read 0, so a shot where any reads 1 is discarded. A bit flip on an interior qubit reads 1, and so does a bit flip on the first port before the last gate, which copies it onto an interior qubit. The read cannot see a phase flip, and it adds no gate and no qubit.
#
# For each graph, the two circuits its cases use, each as the repository holds it, then as the workbench shows it: the graph with the couplers the circuit uses, the written circuit and its cost, then two dropdowns: a step-through of the written circuit with no noise, and the compiled circuits, with a dropdown for each basis holding its compiled circuit, a timeline of when each qubit is busy, and the circuit as OpenQASM 3 (challenge material 02). Each view compiles its circuit here, as a run does, at optimisation level 1 into Boston's native gates (CZ, √X, X and Rz), without simulating.

