# **On every graph the protected case beats the noisy baseline on F while using fewer two-qubit gates, no SWAP and no extra qubit; its only added cost is the shots the read discards.** For each graph, a table sets the noisy baseline, the transport alone (made here with the nine cases' shots and seed, so the same seeds as its run in section 6) and the protected case side by side on the challenge's five measures, then the workbench draws them: a table and a bar per run of its F, and a second bar for a run's kept shots, with its acceptance. The baseline's and the protected case's circuits are in section {{ sec("s4-4") }}; each graph's block below writes out the transport alone, GHZ disentangling without the read, and makes it. Each difference is given with how many combined standard errors it spans.

# %%
wb = Workbench(noise=NOISE, shots=SHOTS, base_seed=BASE_SEED)  # the nine cases' settings
ALONE = {}  # the transport alone on each graph, as made here


def side_by_side(processor):
    """Make the transport alone on one graph, keep its run beside the others, and compare it with the noisy baseline
    and the protected case as the workbench does."""
    baseline, protected = {{ "{" }}{% for p in P %}"{{ p }}": ("{{ base[p].run }}", "{{ prot[p].run }}"){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{{ "}" }}[processor]
    ALONE[processor] = wb.run(f"ghz-uncompute-{processor}")
    ALONE[processor].run.write(folder=MADE / "transport-alone" / f"ghz-uncompute-{processor}")
    here = made(REPORTED)
    return wb.compare(Result.from_folder(here / baseline), ALONE[processor], Result.from_folder(here / protected))
